```{r setup, include = FALSE}
stopifnot(require(knitr))
# seed = in bnec() governs Stan's sampler only. The initial values bayesnec
# generates are drawn in R, so a set.seed() here is required as well before the
# vignette is reproducible. See vignette("example1").
set.seed(17)
knitr::opts_chunk$set(
  collapse = TRUE,
  comment = "#>",
  warning = FALSE,
  message = FALSE,
  fig.path = "vignette-fig-",
  fig.asp = 0.8,
  out.width = "60%",
  fig.align = "center",
  dev = "png",
  dpi = 150,
  eval = identical(Sys.getenv("NOT_CRAN"), "true")
)
```

# Overview

The other vignettes each explain one part of `bayesnec`: what the model set is,
what a `bayesmanecfit` is, how priors are built, how to handle a two-block
response. This one runs a single analysis from data to reportable estimate, in
the order the steps occur.

The workflow is:

1. Prepare the data and choose the family.
2. Fit the candidate set.
3. Check the sampler, and screen out the models that did not sample.
4. Check the fit of the models that remain.
5. Report.

A prior check belongs between steps 2 and 3; [`example3`](https://open-aims.github.io/bayesnec/articles/example3.html) covers
`check_priors()` and the construction of the defaults, and this vignette does
not repeat it.

[`example2`](https://open-aims.github.io/bayesnec/articles/example2.html) notes that there is little harm in leaving a poorly
*shaped* model in the set with low weight, which is true, but non-convergence is
a different failure. Three datasets are worked below, and the sampler screen of
the screen in step 3 has a different outcome on each. Steps 1 to 5 are worked in
full on the first dataset; the second and third are worked only where they
differ from it.

The threshold estimates themselves --- *NEC*, *NSEC*, ECx, and the
model-averaged *N(S)EC* reported when a set mixes both kinds --- are defined in
[`example5`](https://open-aims.github.io/bayesnec/articles/example5.html), and the candidate equations in [`example2b`](https://open-aims.github.io/bayesnec/articles/example2b.html).

```{r libraries}
library(bayesnec)
library(ggplot2)
```

# 1. Data preparation and family choice

The `nassarius` data are survival counts for the tropical marine snail
*Nassarius dorsatus* exposed to each of four contaminants, recorded per
individual within replicate tanks [@trenfield2016]. `?nassarius` documents the
columns and the provenance of the response.

```{r data}
data(nassarius)
head(nassarius)
```

The `record` column matters here. Deaths in this experiment were not recorded
as zeros: they appear as a sentinel value, a blank replicate, or a tank or dose
missing from the file altogether, and `?nassarius` describes how each was
reinstated. For contaminant A every zero-survival record is reinstated rather
than measured.

```{r provenance}
a <- nassarius[nassarius$contaminant == "A", ]
table(a$record, a$alive)
```

Every dose at which anything died is largely or wholly reconstructed, and the
highest dose entirely so, which means the descending limb that determines the
reported estimate rests on it. That is a fact about the data rather than about
`bayesnec`, and it belongs in the methods section of any analysis built on it.
[`example6`](https://open-aims.github.io/bayesnec/articles/example6.html) runs the corresponding sensitivity analysis on
contaminant B, refitting without the reinstated doses to test how much the
result depends on them.

Survival is binary per individual, so the natural unit for a
concentration-response model is the tank: the number alive out of the number
exposed.

```{r aggregate}
surv <- aggregate(alive ~ dose + tank + contaminant,
                  data = nassarius, FUN = function(x) c(suc = sum(x),
                                                        tot = length(x)))
surv <- do.call(data.frame, surv)
names(surv)[names(surv) == "alive.suc"] <- "suc"
names(surv)[names(surv) == "alive.tot"] <- "tot"
surv <- surv[surv$contaminant == "A", ]
head(surv)
```

Look at the data before fitting anything.

```{r exmp9-data}
ggplot(surv, aes(x = dose, y = suc / tot)) +
  geom_point(size = 2, alpha = 0.7) +
  labs(x = "Dose", y = "Proportion surviving") +
  theme_classic()
```

Survival is at or near complete for most of the series and falls away over the
top two doses. Two features carry into what follows: the response is bounded at
1 and sits against that bound for most of the range, and the doses are unevenly
spaced, with most of them crowded near zero.

The dose is left untransformed. [`example1`](https://open-aims.github.io/bayesnec/articles/example1.html) sets out when a
logarithmic predictor helps; here the series already includes a zero control,
and the two herbicide fits later in this vignette show the transformed case.

## The dispersion screen

The default family for successes out of trials is `binomial`, which fixes the
variance at $np(1-p)$. Toxicity data often vary by more than that, for any of
several reasons: animals within a replicate share conditions, so the effective
sample size is smaller than the number of individuals; individuals differ in
susceptibility, so the probability of survival is not one number but a
distribution; and covariates that were not measured contribute the same way. The
`beta_binomial` is the second of those written down explicitly --- `p` varies
between units, drawn from a beta --- and it accommodates the others in practice.
Fitting the whole candidate set under a family that cannot represent the spread
commits every model in it to an assumption the data do not support.

The asymmetry is worth stating: mixing `p` over a beta can only *add* variance,
so `beta_binomial` addresses over-dispersion and does not address the opposite
case. A screen pointing below 1 indicates something about the fit, the design or
the data-generating process rather than about the family.

The dispersion screen requires one model rather than the whole set.

```{r screen, results = "hide"}
screen <- bnec(suc | trials(tot) ~ crf(dose, model = "nec3param"),
               data = surv, family = binomial(link = "identity"),
               iter = 5e3, chains = 3, seed = 17)
```

`bnec()` sets `link = "identity"` by default on every family, so that `top`,
`bot` and `nec` are estimated on the scale of the response and remain directly
interpretable. It is written out above for clarity; passing the family as a
bare string has the same effect. A user who supplies a family object with a
different link --- `binomial(link = "logit")` --- overrides that default, and
the model then fits a non-linear curve to the logit of the mean rather than to
the mean. `bnec()` accommodates this by dropping the equations that are invalid
on a link scale, but the parameters are no longer on the response scale. See
[`example1`](https://open-aims.github.io/bayesnec/articles/example1.html).

```{r screen-disp}
dispersion(screen, summary = TRUE)
```

`dispersion()` returns, for each posterior draw, the ratio of the observed to
the simulated sum of squared Pearson residuals, summarised here to a median and
an equal-tailed 95% interval. The statistic centres near 1 when the family's
variance assumption holds. Because a posterior is returned rather than a point,
the decision is read from the **interval** rather than from the point estimate,
which carries no information about how well determined the ratio is.

```{r family-choice}
disp <- dispersion(screen, summary = TRUE)
# length(disp) guards the case below: dispersion() returns an empty vector where
# the fitted mean reaches 0 or 1, and there is then no verdict to read.
fam <- if (length(disp) && disp[["Q2.5"]] > 1) "beta_binomial" else "binomial"
fam
```

The interval runs from `r round(disp[["Q2.5"]], 2)` to
`r round(disp[["Q97.5"]], 2)`. It includes 1, so the screen provides no evidence
against the binomial variance assumption and the analysis proceeds under
`binomial`. Had the lower bound exceeded 1, `beta_binomial` would be the choice.
The point estimate is `r round(disp[["Estimate"]], 2)`: thresholding that number
would decide the family on a quantity the screen has just shown to be poorly
determined, since `r nrow(surv)` tanks across
`r length(unique(surv$dose))` doses do not pin the ratio down.

**The dispersion screen detects only gross over-dispersion.** Each draw compares
one observed residual
sum against a single simulated replicate, so the width of the interval is
dominated by replicate-to-replicate simulation noise rather than by uncertainty
about a dispersion parameter. At this design an interval of this width is close
to what a correctly specified binomial produces on its own, which means the
`Q2.5 > 1` rule fires only on gross over-dispersion. It is the right shape of
rule --- a threshold on the point estimate answers a question the data cannot
support --- but it is a blunt one, and a screen that returns `binomial` is
better read as *not having detected* over-dispersion than as evidence of its
absence.

`dispersion()` is defined only for `poisson` and `binomial`, the two families
whose variance is fixed by the mean, and returns an empty vector otherwise.
[`example1`](https://open-aims.github.io/bayesnec/articles/example1.html) reads over-dispersion on both, and works the
`binomial` to `beta_binomial` and `poisson` to `negbinomial` switches that this
dataset does not require.

This is a pre-check: it asks whether the data are broadly consistent with the
family under consideration before the compute is committed to a full model set.
It is also model-conditional --- it reads dispersion from one shape, and a
dispersion ratio cannot separate over-dispersion from a shape that fits badly
--- so the screening fit has to pass the sampler checks itself before its
verdict is used. The same question is asked again of the fitted set in step 2,
where every candidate reports its own dispersion.

```{r screen-sampling}
check_sampling(screen)
```

This fit passes all three cutoffs, which is what makes its verdict usable.
`nec3param` was chosen over the four-parameter form for that reason: on these
data `nec4param` reaches an acceptable Rhat and effective sample size once given
10,000 iterations at `adapt_delta = 0.99`, but still returns of the order of a
hundred divergent transitions, and the screen in step 3 removes it. Its
dispersion verdict is the same, so the family decision does not turn on the
choice of shape --- but a verdict read from a fit that has not sampled is not
evidence, and that has to be established rather than assumed.

# 2. Fitting the candidate set

```{r fit, results = "hide"}
fit <- bnec(suc | trials(tot) ~ crf(dose, model = "decline"),
            data = surv, family = fam,
            iter = 5e3, chains = 3, seed = 17)
```

`bnec()` defaults to `chains = 4` and `iter = 1e4`, which retains 8000 draws;
the fits here retain 3000, to keep the vignette's runtime within what a package
build allows. That matters in step 3, where the effective sample size is
screened against a cutoff calibrated to the default.

Two arguments matter once a fit is marginal:

- `iter` and `warmup`. A model with a poorly identified `nec` parameter may need
  more draws. Raise `iter` first when Rhat is marginal.
- `control = list(adapt_delta = 0.99, max_treedepth = 12)`. Raise `adapt_delta`
  when there are divergent transitions; raise `max_treedepth` when the sampler
  reports hitting it. Both increase runtime and neither repairs a misspecified
  model.

## The set as fitted

`model = "decline"` requests `r length(models()$decline)` equations, and fewer
than that are averaged over. Two mechanisms reduce the set: some equations are
excluded before fitting because they are invalid for the family or the
predictor --- [`example2b`](https://open-aims.github.io/bayesnec/articles/example2b.html) sets out the equations and the rules that
govern them --- and others are attempted and fail.

```{r set-fitted}
failed <- names(failed_models(fit))
list(fitted = fit$success_models,
     failed = failed,
     excluded = setdiff(models()$decline, c(fit$success_models, failed)))
```

`neclin` and `ecxlin` decay by subtraction rather than by an exponential factor,
so their fitted mean is unbounded below and cannot be held inside the (0, 1)
interval a `binomial` mean occupies under an identity link. `bnec()` drops them
and issues a message at fit time, suppressed here along with the sampler output.
`?models` sets out this rule and the others; a second one applies to the
herbicide fits later, where the predictor is transformed.

## The fitted set

`summary()` reports the set as a whole, and two of its columns carry the
workflow forward.

```{r summary-fit}
summary(fit)
```

`wi` is the model weight: the share of the model-averaged prediction each
candidate contributes, summing to one across the set. The weights come from
PSIS-LOO --- `loo_model_weights()` consumes the `loo` objects `brms` computes
for each fit --- and on these data several models report Pareto *k* values above
0.7 for a few observations, which `brms` warns about and this vignette's chunk
options suppress. The weights are usable and the estimates behind them are
flagged as unreliable at those points, which belongs in a methods statement
alongside the weights themselves.

`dispersion_Estimate` and its interval are the pre-fit screen of section 1,
asked again of every candidate now that the set exists. This is where the
question is properly answered, because the screen was one shape and this is all
of them. Two things to read from it: whether the models carrying weight agree
with the screen, and whether any of them report `NA`.

```{r na-dispersion}
ms <- fit$mod_stats[, c("model", "wi", "dispersion_Estimate")]
ms[is.na(ms$dispersion_Estimate), ]
```

`dispersion()` builds its Pearson denominator from each model's *fitted* mean,
so a candidate whose fitted mean reaches 0 or 1 has no denominator and the
statistic is undefined. It is a property of that candidate rather than of the
data, which is why only some report it. Where the models carrying the weight are
the ones returning `NA`, the dispersion screen cannot speak to the family at
all, and the family decision rests on the local checks in section 4 instead.

Models that fail to fit are recorded rather than silently discarded:

```{r failed}
failed_models(fit)
```

The priors and initial values the failed attempt was given are retained because
they are built inside `bnec()` and are otherwise unrecoverable. A retry with
those priors unchanged reproduces the failure; they are the starting point for
an adjustment, as `?failed_models` sets out:

```{r retry, eval = FALSE}
f <- failed_models(fit)$ecxwb1
f$prior                                   # the priors that failed
# Widen the prior that pinned the fit, then retry with the edited set.
new_prior <- f$prior
new_prior$prior[new_prior$nlpar == "beta"] <- "normal(0, 5)"
bnec(suc | trials(tot) ~ crf(dose, model = "ecxwb1"), data = surv,
     family = fam, prior = new_prior)
```

[`example3`](https://open-aims.github.io/bayesnec/articles/example3.html) covers how the defaults are built and what a sensible
adjustment looks like.

# 3. Sampler diagnostics and screening

The first question is whether the sampler worked. Whether the model fits is
step 4. This section ends by removing the candidates that did not sample, so
that everything after it concerns a set worth averaging.

`check_sampling()` reports per candidate model, so the whole set is screened in
one call rather than model by model.

```{r sampling}
check_sampling(fit)
```

The table gives one row per fitted model, with three diagnostics: the largest
Rhat, the smallest effective sample size, and the number of divergent
transitions.

All three cutoffs are arguments. The Rhat default of 1.01 and the ESS default of
400 follow published recommendations [@Vehtari2021]; **the divergence default of
10 does not**. Stan's own guidance is that *any* divergence means the sampler
failed to explore the posterior. The default of ten is a package convention
rather than a published recommendation: these non-linear models commonly
produce a small number of divergent transitions near a parameter boundary, and
`?check_sampling` says the same. The value 1.01 is the `bayesnec` default
throughout: `rhat()`, `summary()` and `check_sampling()` agree on it.

Read `min_ess` rather than `min_ess_ratio` when deciding. The 400 is an absolute
floor, below which the Rhat and ESS estimates are themselves unreliable, and it
corresponds to 100 draws per chain at the four chains `bnec()` fits by default.
These fits retain 3000 draws rather than 8000, so a model can fail on `min_ess`
at a ratio that would pass at the default. The response to that is more draws,
not a lower cutoff --- thinning or short runs lower ESS by construction, and
`?check_sampling` makes the same point. The ratio is reported beside the count
because it answers a different question from the one the cutoff answers: it
separates a model that cleared 400 because many draws were taken from one that
cleared it efficiently, and it is what
reveals a heavily thinned fit. `?check_sampling` sets out the distinction. The
decision is taken on `min_ess`; the ratio is read alongside it.

Chain mixing has to be inspected as well, and for every model rather than for
the worst one. Poor mixing occurs at an acceptable Rhat, and there is no
established scalar that catches what rank-normalised split-Rhat and bulk and
tail effective sample size miss. `check_chains()` takes a filename and writes
one page per model, which keeps the output out of a report while leaving a
record that can be inspected and archived:

```{r chains, eval = FALSE}
check_chains(fit, filename = "nassarius_check_chains")
```

What to look for: chains overlapping one another across the whole trace, with no
drift and no excursion that one chain makes and the others do not. A chain that
wanders into a region the others never visit is the visual form of a failed
Rhat, and a chain that moves slowly through the same region is the visual form
of a low effective sample size.

## Screening the model set

Model averaging combines the candidates in proportion to their weights, so a
model that has not sampled contributes to the reported estimate on the strength
of a posterior the sampler never explored. Only models that pass the sampler
diagnostics should be carried forward.

```{r exclude, message = TRUE}
fit_screened <- screen_models(fit)
```

`screen_models()` runs the same diagnostics as the table above, drops what
failed, and **reports which models were removed and why**: one line per model,
naming every threshold it missed rather than only the first. An exclusion step
that leaves no record is not reproducible.

It screens on the **sampler only**. Whether a model reproduces the data is a
modelling result rather than a reason the fit is unusable, and dropping on it
silently would hide what section 4 exists to show.

If every candidate fails, `screen_models()` stops with an error listing the
reasons rather than returning a meaningless average, which signals a return to
section 2 rather than a loosened threshold. If none fails, it says so and
returns the set unchanged.

`amend()` does the work, recomputing the weights over the reduced set, and it is
the only route by which a model leaves a `bayesmanecfit`. The weights of a
screened set therefore have to be read from the screened fit rather than
rescaled by hand.

The justification for the step is that a model which has not sampled should not
contribute to an averaged estimate, not that removing it changes the answer.
Whether it does is a property of the particular dataset, and is not knowable
before the screen is run.

```{r exmp9-plot-after}
autoplot(fit_screened)
```

The averaged curve with its credible band, over the screened set. The band
should contain the bulk of the observations and widen where the data are sparse;
a band that excludes whole groups is a fit problem rather than a sampler one,
and belongs to section 4.

Some retained models carry divergent transitions below the tolerated ten. The
threshold used here admits them, which is the kind of decision the reporting
section exists to make explicit.

# 4. Fit diagnostics

The sampler diagnostics indicate whether the sampler explored the posterior
adequately. They say nothing about whether the model fits. Two checks answer
different questions: `pp_check()` overall, and `check_fit()` within
concentration groups.

The checks below are worked on the highest-weighted model of the screened set,
which is the candidate contributing most to the reported estimate and therefore
the one whose fit matters most. `pull_out()` extracts it as a `bayesnecfit`.

If the screen leaves a single model, `screen_models()` returns a `bayesnecfit`
directly rather than a one-model set, so code that reads `$mod_stats` has to
allow for it. That is documented behaviour rather than an edge case: a set of
one is not a model average, and the object says so.

```{r best-model}
# A screened set reduced to one model is returned as a bayesnecfit rather than a
# bayesmanecfit, and carries no mod_stats -- see ?screen_models.
best_fit <- if (inherits(fit_screened, "bayesmanecfit")) {
  ms <- fit_screened$mod_stats
  pull_out(fit_screened, model = ms$model[which.max(ms$wi)])
} else {
  fit_screened
}
best_fit$model
```

`pp_check()` asks whether data simulated from the fitted model resemble the data
observed. The response is a bounded count, so `type = "bars"` is the appropriate
display; the default density overlay smooths a discrete distribution. Pooled
over the whole dataset the comparison is weak for concentration-response data,
because a model can match the pooled distribution while being wrong at every
concentration, so it is grouped by the predictor here:

```{r exmp9-ppcheck}
pp_check(best_fit, type = "bars_grouped", group = "dose")
```

The observed counts are the dark points and the simulated distribution the light
intervals, one panel per concentration. A panel whose observed point sits
outside its interval is a concentration at which the model does not generate the
observed counts at the right frequency.

`check_fit()` tests something narrower: whether the model reproduces the
observed variability and the observed level *locally*, within each concentration
group, with the control flagged.

**The control is the lowest value of the predictor.** That is the package's
convention throughout: `check_fit()` flags that group, and `nsec()` reads its
reference from the posterior of the fitted mean there. An experiment whose
lowest treatment is not a control is being analysed on that assumption and
should say so.

For a `bayesmanecfit` it returns every group of every candidate model, which is
more than can be read at once. Two views are useful. The first is the control
row of each model, which is the row that matters most: `nsec()` sets its
reference from the posterior of the fitted control mean, so control variability
propagates into a reported no-effect concentration.

```{r checkfit-control}
cf <- check_fit(fit_screened)
cf[cf$control, ]
```

The control group is 0.001 rather than 0 because `bnec()` moves a zero predictor
to one tenth of the smallest positive value: a `Gamma`-distributed predictor
cannot take the value zero. The substitution is the same idea as the response
substitution described later for a `Beta` response, applied to the other axis.

**The flagged column is `ppp_mean`, and the discrepancy is one of location.**
The fitted control mean is 5.75 against an observed 5.25, because the groups
immediately above the control are all at complete survival and pull `top` up.
That single fact produces both the high `ppp_mean` and the low `ppp_sd`.

The consequence is worth stating because `nsec()` reads its reference from the
posterior of the fitted control mean. A control mean simulated above the
observed one raises that reference, so the curve crosses it earlier and the
no-effect estimate moves in the protective direction. Every candidate shows it,
so model averaging does not remove it.

The `sd_ratio` of 1.9 is not by itself evidence of over-dispersion. `sim_sd` is
the *median* over draws of a residual standard deviation computed from four
replicates, and at that size the statistic takes only a handful of distinct
values, so the ratio is confined to a short ladder rather than varying
continuously. The column that carries the evidence is `ppp_sd`, and at 0.06 to
0.09 it places the observed spread in the upper tail of what the model
simulates, inside the interval `check_fit()` flags on but not far from its edge,
on a single group of four tanks. That is a weak signal. Reading "twice the
spread" off the ratio would overstate it, in the same way that reading the
family off a point estimate would in step 1.

The second view is the full table for a single model, read when one candidate is
in question:

```{r checkfit-one}
check_fit(best_fit)
```

Two columns need care on a binomial response with few replicates.

- `sd_ratio` is `obs_sd` divided by the *median* simulated residual standard
  deviation. With three or four replicates that median is often exactly zero,
  which is what produces `Inf`, and exactly zero observed spread produces 0,
  and both together `NaN`. A zero median does not mean the model simulates no
  spread: at the second row `ppp_sd` is near 0.5, so about half the draws
  simulated more spread than was observed. Read `ppp_sd` and the plot rather
  than the ratio.
- The posterior predictive p-value is `mean(sim >= obs)`. A value of 1.000
  arises where the observed statistic sits at the floor of its range --- an
  observed standard deviation of zero, or an observed mean of zero at the
  highest dose --- so every simulated value equals or exceeds it. That marks a
  statistic with no room to move, not a misfitted group.

```{r exmp9-checkfit-plot}
plot(check_fit(best_fit))
```

The plot shows what the table does not: the magnitude and direction of any
discrepancy. Each panel gives the observed statistic against the 95% span of
what the model simulates; a point outside its bar is a group the model does not
reproduce, subject to the same caution about groups with no observed spread.
Location and scale are separate panels because they fail independently. A model
can reproduce every group's mean while simulating far too much spread, which is
the discrepancy this check is designed to detect.

# 5. Reporting

What to state in a methods section:

- **the data as analysed**, including any reconstructed records and which part
  of the concentration-response they carry;
- **the family, and how it was chosen** --- the dispersion screen, its interval,
  the shape it was read from, and what the local check in step 4 showed;
- **the candidate set as fitted**: the set requested, the equations excluded as
  invalid, and any model that failed to fit at all, with a note of whether a
  retry was attempted;
- **the sampler settings**, including the number of retained draws, since the
  ESS cutoff is calibrated against a default this analysis did not use;
- **the diagnostic thresholds used** --- all three, since they are choices and
  they determine what is excluded. `check_sampling()`'s defaults are 1.01, 400
  and 10; state whether any were changed, and why;
- **which models were excluded and why**, with the weights before and after, so
  that a reader can see what the screen did rather than accept it without
  evidence;
- **the estimate settings**, which determine the numbers as much as the model
  set does: `sig_val`, which sets the *NSEC* reference from the control
  posterior and defaults to 0.01; `type` for `ecx()`, defaulting to
  `"absolute"`; and any `xform` used to return an estimate to the measured
  scale;
- **the estimates with their credible intervals**, and the weights of the
  retained models.

```{r report}
summary(fit_screened)
```

`summary()` reports the model-averaged *N(S)EC*: the set mixes threshold and
smooth equations, so the averaged estimate is a mixture of *NEC* and *NSEC*
draws and should not be reported as a *NEC*. ECx values are the other commonly
required endpoint, read from the same screened set:

```{r report-ecx}
ecx(fit_screened, ecx_val = 10)
ecx(fit_screened, ecx_val = 50)
```

Both are model-averaged over the screened set and reported with credible
intervals, on the scale of the predictor as fitted. `type` defaults to
`"absolute"`, measuring the decline against the fitted control rather than
against the range of the curve, and it changes the number materially ---
[`example5`](https://open-aims.github.io/bayesnec/articles/example5.html) sets out the difference.

# Two further datasets

## A continuous response

The screen in step 1 and the checks in step 4 behave differently for a
continuous response. The `herbicide` data record photosynthetic yield, a
proportion bounded by 0 and 1 but not a count, which is the `beta` case.

`simazine` is chosen for a reason that applies to any Beta response. A `Beta`
response is supported on the open interval (0, 1), so a value recorded as
exactly 0 or 1 lies outside the family's support. `bayesnec` substitutes for
both: a zero becomes one tenth of the smallest non-zero value, which is relative
to the scale of the response, and a one is reduced by an absolute 0.001. A
series that spends several concentrations at a boundary is then being modelled
through that substitution rather than as recorded. `simazine` spans 0.16--0.71
and reaches neither boundary. Several of the other herbicides in this dataset
do: `irgarol` records ten zeros, eight of its twelve replicates at the highest
concentration, so the family for these data depends on inspecting the response
first. Where zeros are part of the process rather than a recording floor, see
[`example6`](https://open-aims.github.io/bayesnec/articles/example6.html), which covers the families that model them explicitly.

```{r herbicide, results = "hide"}
data(herbicide)
simazine <- herbicide[herbicide$herbicide == "simazine", ]
fit_b <- bnec(fvfm ~ crf(log(concentration), model = "decline"),
              data = simazine, family = Beta(link = "identity"),
              iter = 5e3, chains = 3, seed = 17)
```

Two differences follow.

- **There is no family choice to make.** `beta` already carries a free
  dispersion parameter (`phi`), so the over-dispersion screen has nothing to
  decide. The screen in step 1 is specifically a `binomial` and `poisson`
  question.
- **`check_fit()` is more informative here**, because a beta response has
  genuine residual variability at every concentration, where a binomial response
  near total mortality has almost none and every group appears adequately fitted
  by construction.

The candidate set also differs, and the same accounting shows it:

```{r herbicide-account}
list(fitted = fit_b$success_models,
     excluded = setdiff(models()$decline, fit_b$success_models))
```

A third equation is excluded here. The predictor is `log(concentration)`, and
`concentration` starts at 0.1, so the transformed predictor is negative;
`bnec()` drops the equations that raise the predictor to a fractional power,
which cannot be evaluated below zero. `ecxsigm` is among them, and it was the
highest-weighted model of the `nassarius` set --- the same requested set gives
different candidates on a transformed predictor.

```{r herbicide-check}
cf_b <- check_fit(fit_b)
cf_b[cf_b$control, ]
```

This table shows the discrepancy `check_fit()` exists to detect, and one the
binomial groups above could not exhibit. `ecxexp` reproduces the
control mean to within about a tenth while simulating several times the observed
control spread: the location is right and the scale is not. Its weight is
negligible, so the discrepancy does not propagate into the averaged estimate.
`?check_fit` records the opposite case, in which a model carries high weight
because it fits the bulk of the curve while fitting the control badly.

```{r herbicide-screen, message = TRUE}
fit_b_screened <- screen_models(fit_b)
```

The predictor was logged in the formula, so the threshold is returned on that
scale, and `nec()`, `nsec()` and `ecx()` all take an `xform` argument to put it
back on the measured one.

```{r herbicide-scale}
rbind(log_scale = nec(fit_b_screened),
      measured = nec(fit_b_screened, xform = exp))
```

Both rows are plausible as concentrations in a series running from 0.1 to 300,
which is why a reported value has to state its scale. Only the second is one.

## A set that passes unchanged

`diuron` gives the third outcome.

```{r diuron, results = "hide"}
diuron <- herbicide[herbicide$herbicide == "diuron", ]
fit_c <- bnec(fvfm ~ crf(log(concentration), model = "decline"),
              data = diuron, family = Beta(link = "identity"),
              iter = 5e3, chains = 3, seed = 17)
```

```{r diuron-screen, message = TRUE}
check_sampling(fit_c)
fit_c_screened <- screen_models(fit_c)
```

No candidate fails at these thresholds, and `screen_models()` returns the set
unchanged. One model carries four divergent transitions, below the tolerated
ten. That outcome is itself a result to report: the methods statement is that
the screen was applied at stated thresholds and removed nothing, which is a
stronger claim than silence about whether it was applied at all.

# Summary

Across the three datasets the sampler screen removed half the model weight and
moved nothing, removed a tenth and moved the estimate by about a percent, and
removed nothing at all. Which of those a given analysis produces is not
predictable from the weights, and the reportable result is the screen and its
effect rather than a movement assumed in advance.

The same holds one level up. The dispersion screen returned `binomial` because
it detected nothing, not because nothing was there; the fit checks found a
control the models place above the observed one, in a direction that is
protective; and the estimates are reported on the scale they were fitted on
unless `xform` says otherwise. None of those is a result the workflow produces
automatically. Each is a decision the analysis has to record.

